---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import DayStop from '../components/DayStop.astro';
import Kiosk from '../components/Kiosk.astro';
import LoungeBoard from '../components/LoungeBoard.astro';
import DeviceRace from '../components/DeviceRace.astro';
import DoctorFlow from '../components/DoctorFlow.astro';
import FloorClock from '../components/FloorClock.astro';
import FloorMap from '../components/FloorMap.astro';
import NightlyReport from '../components/NightlyReport.astro';
import Numbers from '../components/Numbers.astro';
import HowIWork from '../components/HowIWork.astro';
import Questions from '../components/Questions.astro';
import Contact from '../components/Contact.astro';
---
<Base>
  <Hero />

  <!-- One clinic day in seven parts. Each part: one bold claim with a number, one grey line, one screen.
       nextFlip tells the squiggle which side the next part's numeral is on. -->
  <div id="day" style="scroll-margin-top: var(--bar-h)">

    <DayStop id="checkin" n={1} time="Front desk" nextFlip={false}>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        A patient walks in and checks in at a kiosk. They drop in their card, and the EMR checks them in on
        its own. Every check-in hands the front desk 35 seconds back. At this clinic's 380 patients a day,
        that is 3 hours and 40 minutes, every day.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        It reads the person's details off whatever card they drop in, inserted any direction. A health card
        is verified on the spot, and if it is new, the file is updated automatically. No typing, no line at
        the desk. Foolproof.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><Kiosk /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="boards" n={2} time="The TVs on the wall" wide nextFlip={false}>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        Every TV in the clinic shows who is next and how long they have waited. Doctors, techs and the front
        desk all see the same picture, so nobody walks the hall to ask, and nobody is left waiting in a room.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        One view per role, on the TVs the clinic already had. Move a patient along on one and all the others
        follow, with the EMR updated at the same time. Open it on any device the clinic wants.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><LoungeBoard /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="devices" n={3} time="Imaging room" nextFlip={true}>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        Test results go straight from the machine into the patient's chart: half a second instead of the two
        minutes it takes to type them, with zero typos. On just three of this clinic's machines, that is
        another 1 hour and 50 minutes a day.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        Every machine feeds the chart the same way. The tech finishes the test and walks the patient to the
        next room. The numbers are already in the chart, exactly as measured.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><DeviceRace /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="doctor" n={4} time="Exam room" flip nextFlip={false}>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        The doctor taps the patient's name once, and the chart, the scans and the photos open across two
        monitors. On average, 27 seconds back on every patient. Across 380 patients, that is 2 hours and
        50 minutes of doctor time a day.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        Always the right patient, and the right view for that visit: a scan review brings up the scan, a
        glaucoma visit brings up the field test.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><DoctorFlow /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="staff" n={5} time="Staff room" nextFlip={true}>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        The staff schedule and the time clock live on one screen in the staff room. The manager runs the
        floor from the office, and payroll that used to take an afternoon every two weeks now takes no time
        at all.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        Staff clock in and out with a personal code. The manager gets a morning and an end-of-day summary,
        and the pay-period timesheet arrives with the totals done.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><FloorClock /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="map" n={6} time="Anywhere in the clinic" flip nextFlip={false}>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        A live map of the clinic. Every patient is a dot moving from the front desk to the exam room,
        colour-coded by stage. Anyone on staff sees the whole clinic in one glance.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        The same colours as the screens. An empty room with people waiting stands out instantly.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><FloorMap /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="nightly" n={7} time="The manager's inbox" last>
      <Fragment slot="claim">
        Every night the day is reviewed, and a short note lands in the manager's inbox: where the day slowed
        down, and what to change tomorrow. The clinic gets faster every week.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="detail">
        One system watching the whole clinic builds a far more detailed record than anyone could keep by
        hand, and the machine-learning model trained on it gets sharper every day: where each staff member
        performs best, which schedule brings in the most, where the bottlenecks form, and metrics you would
        never think to ask for, found daily and handed to you. Nobody types any of it, and the staff always
        make the final call.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual"><NightlyReport /></Fragment>
    </DayStop>
  </div>

  <Numbers />
  <HowIWork />
  <Questions />
  <Contact />
</Base>
